class:: DynamicComplexity
summary:: Variation in RMS frame levels around average power
categories:: UGens>Analysis
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/Amplitude

description::
Computes the root mean square dB distance of nonsilent RMS frames from the power-mean level of the entire window. Frames at or below floorDB are excluded from the variation average. This explicitly defined MIR2 dynamic-variation statistic is independently implemented and does not claim numerical equivalence with Essentia DynamicComplexity.

Frames contain nonoverlapping RMS measurements. Output stays zero until a complete observation window is available, then updates once per RMS frame. Silence returns zero. Runtime cost is linear in windowTime/frameTime per update; there is no callback allocation. These descriptors are rolling summaries and do not segment individual events. Formulas are independently implemented from standard weighted moments and linear regression.

The nearest vanilla building block is link::Classes/Amplitude::, which tracks instantaneous level; this UGen summarizes its recent distribution in time.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: in
Audio-rate signal.

argument:: windowTime
Initialization-time observation duration, 2*frameTime through 10 seconds. Rounded up to whole RMS frames.

argument:: frameTime
Initialization-time RMS frame duration, 0.001 through 0.1 seconds.

argument:: floorDB
Silence floor in dBFS, clipped to -160..0. Used by dynamic complexity and dB regression; temporal moments use the original energy.

returns::
Nonnegative level variation in dB.

examples::
DynamicComplexity computes the root-mean-square dB variation of non-silent RMS frames around the power-mean level of the rolling observation window. Steady continuous sounds produce low dynamic complexity (< 4 dB), while dynamic rhythmic grooves and articulated percussion produce high complexity (> 10 dB). In this example, dynamic complexity introduces a subtle granular delay scatter layer during highly dynamic passages. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time dynamic complexity in dB.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var dynComp = DynamicComplexity.kr(source, windowTime: 0.5, frameTime: 0.005);
        var compLag = dynComp.lag(0.08);
        var scatterSend = compLag.linlin(4, 14, 0.0, 0.4).clip(0.0, 0.5);
        var scatter = CombC.ar(source, 0.1, SinOsc.kr([0.4, 0.5]).range(0.02, 0.06), 0.5) * scatterSend;
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + scatter;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, compLag.clip(0, 24)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Dynamic Complexity", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 20.0, "dB");
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

